I built the Brief A agent and ran four test passes. Only 7 of the 10 practice customers passed the last run. Most failures in the last three passes came from an outage in the platform's code sandbox ("snekbox"), which runs the agent's code nodes and tool hooks. It was unreachable for a minute or two several times.

**How the agent works:** an opening step, then the security questions, then the account is loaded and code works out the deal terms, then the payment talk, then a closing step. Code handles all the date and amount rules: business days, bank holidays, the deadline for each lateness band, the lowest amount allowed, the persuasion attempts, the callback window, and a table of which promise dates are allowed. Code also blocks a promise or callback that breaks the brief before it reaches the bank. The agent only moves to the next step on a tool result.

**Results:**

| Pass | Passed | What went wrong |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 8/10 | Both failures were sandbox outages |
| 2 | 8/10 | Bank records all correct. The judge failed PUB-10 because the opening said "This call is recorded", and PUB-06 because the agent offered the minimum payment twice. I fixed both. |
| 3 | 5/10 | All 5 failures were sandbox outages. Every call outside the outage window passed. |
| 4 | 7/10 | PUB-01 and PUB-04 failed because the code steps were down: the agent spoke raw placeholders and recorded a date past the deadline. PUB-03 failed because of my own change. |

For pass 4 I had taken the code hooks off the identity check, transfer and close tools to reduce exposure to the outage. That caused PUB-03 to fail: the agent treated a wrong date of birth as correct. I put those hooks back and saved the result as version 5. That version is the one on the platform now, and **it has not been tested** because the three fix rounds were used up. Its wiring is the same as the version that passed every call outside the outage window in pass 3.

**Decision for you:** the agent depends on the sandbox for its code steps and several tools, so a sandbox outage during scoring will fail calls whatever the agent does. Taking that dependency out mostly means moving the date and amount rules back into prompts. Pass 4 showed the agent then stops respecting the identity-check result, which is why I kept the hooks.

The workflow is in `out/brief-a.workflow.json` and is generated by `gen/brief_a.py`; run results are in `results/run-a1.json` to `run-a4.json`.

